<%#
  "Sign in with your wallet" — the WEB3 STEP-UP card.

  WHO SEES IT: Web3StepUpPolicy decides, server-side, at auth success. The
  population is exactly one intersection — an account that holds a self-custody
  wallet (phantom_wallet?) whose CURRENT SESSION was established by a web2
  credential (magic link, email, Google). Both facts are ordinary on their own;
  together they mean the person is signed in but cannot sign anything on-chain.

  HOW IT OPENS: the layout reads the one-shot session[:web3_step_up_prompt] and
  the chain driver opens this BEFORE the onboarding chain, handing off to that
  chain when this card is dismissed.

  DISMISSIBLE by design (operator call, 2026-08-21). Closing it leaves the user
  browsing with their picks intact; every on-chain gate still refuses an
  unsigned session, so dismissal buys nothing it should not. Getting this
  backwards would lock a legitimate owner out of their own account over a wallet
  they merely cannot reach right now — hence the support line at the bottom.

  Props (all optional, from Web3StepUpPolicy#to_h):
    provider      — normalised brand key ('phantom' / 'solflare' / 'backpack'),
                    or null when this account linked its wallet before we
                    started remembering the brand.
    providerLabel — how that brand writes its own name, for the CTA.
    walletHint    — the truncated address (4…4), so the user can confirm we are
                    asking for the wallet they think we are.

  WITH a remembered provider this is ONE button. Without one it is the same card
  with the generic picker as its primary action — the fallback is never a dead
  end, just an extra choice.

  WHAT SIGNING DOES: the CTA runs the ordinary wallet sign-in
  (window.solanaConnectAndVerify -> POST /auth/solana/verify), which
  re-establishes identity FROM THE SIGNATURE and grants session[:onchain].
  Deliberately not linkMode: that posts to /account/link_solana, which binds to
  the current user but never grants the on-chain session — the thing this card
  exists to obtain. One consequence, stated plainly because it is inherited from
  the standalone Solana button rather than introduced here: signing with a
  DIFFERENT wallet signs you into that wallet's account. The walletHint is shown
  precisely so that is a visible choice rather than a surprise.

  CRITICAL (Alpine): this partial is cloned from <template x-if="...">, so it
  must have ONE root element, and the x-data attribute below is double-quoted —
  keep every inner string in SINGLE quotes and use no backticks, or the
  attribute closes early and the whole modal silently mounts as a no-op. The
  gallery test asserts this; it has bitten this codebase twice.
%>
<div x-data="{
       get props() { var c = $store.modals.current(); return (c && c.props) || {}; },
       connecting: false,
       error: '',
       // Which wallets this document can actually see. Read on a poll, NOT once
       // at mount: wallet_provider.js carries an explicit warning that
       // available() fills in asynchronously as wallets register, and this card
       // auto-opens on the render right after auth — the worst possible moment.
       // A single early read is a coin flip that would badge an installed wallet
       // as missing.
       wallets: [],
       _poll: null,
       // Set when the remembered wallet turns out not to be reachable in THIS
       // browser (a different machine, the extension removed). It flips the card
       // to the picker rather than leaving the user pressing a button that
       // cannot work.
       providerMissing: false,
       get provider() { return this.props.provider || null; },
       get providerLabel() { return this.props.providerLabel || null; },
       get walletHint() { return this.props.walletHint || null; },
       // One-click only while we remember a brand AND it is still reachable.
       get canOneClick() { return !!this.provider && !this.providerMissing; },
       // Does the remembered brand answer in THIS document right now? Drives the
       // green Installed badge, the same one the connect picker and the wallet
       // setup row show, so a detected wallet reads identically in all three.
       get detected() {
         var name = ('' + (this.provider || '')).toLowerCase();
         if (!name) return false;
         return (this.wallets || []).some(function(w) { return w.name && w.name.toLowerCase() === name; });
       },
       init() {
         var self = this;
         this.refresh();
         // Registration can land a tick after the module loads, and again later
         // when a locked extension wakes. Free to ask (available() is an array
         // copy), so keep asking until the row has something to show.
         this._onRegister = function() { if (!self.connecting) self.refresh(); };
         window.addEventListener('wallet-standard:register-wallet', this._onRegister);
         this._poll = setInterval(function() {
           if (self.connecting) return;
           self.refresh();
           if (self.wallets.length > 0) { clearInterval(self._poll); self._poll = null; }
         }, 1000);
       },
       destroy() {
         if (this._poll) clearInterval(this._poll);
         if (this._onRegister) window.removeEventListener('wallet-standard:register-wallet', this._onRegister);
       },
       refresh() {
         this.wallets = (window.walletProvider && window.walletProvider.available && window.walletProvider.available()) || [];
       },
       // Is the remembered wallet actually present in this document? Read at
       // CLICK time, never at mount — wallet_provider.js carries an explicit
       // warning that available() fills in asynchronously as wallets register,
       // and this card auto-opens right after auth, the worst possible moment.
       reachable(name) {
         if (!window.walletProvider || !window.walletProvider.get) return false;
         return !!window.walletProvider.get(name);
       },
       async signIn() {
         if (this.connecting) return;
         var name = this.provider;
         if (!name) return this.openPicker();
         if (!this.reachable(name)) {
           this.providerMissing = true;
           this.error = 'We could not reach your ' + (this.providerLabel || 'wallet') + ' in this browser.';
           return;
         }
         this.connecting = true; this.error = '';
         try {
           var result = await window.solanaConnectAndVerify(name, { linkMode: false });
           if (result && result.success) {
             if (typeof window.handleSolanaVerifySuccess === 'function') window.handleSolanaVerifySuccess(result);
             // Reload rather than follow result.redirect: the user was already
             // somewhere, and the only thing that changed is that this session
             // can now sign. Sending them to the post-login landing would move
             // them off the page they were reading.
             window.location.reload();
             return;
           }
           this.error = (result && result.error) || 'Verification failed.';
         } catch (e) {
           var msg = (e && e.code === 4001) ? 'Signature rejected' : ((e && e.message) || 'Connection failed');
           if (typeof parseSolanaError === 'function') msg = parseSolanaError(msg);
           this.error = msg;
         }
         this.connecting = false;
       },
       // Hand off to the full brand picker. swap() rather than open() so the
       // back arrow in that picker returns HERE instead of stranding the user.
       openPicker() {
         if (this.connecting) return;
         $store.modals.swap('wallet-connect', { backTo: 'web3-step-up', stepUpProps: this.props, returnUrl: window.location.href });
       },
       // Dismiss. Dispatched so the onboarding chain driver knows this card is
       // done and can start the chain the user may still owe — the same
       // report-and-close contract the onboarding modal keeps.
       dismiss() {
         if (this.connecting) return;
         window.dispatchEvent(new CustomEvent('web3-step-up-dismissed'));
         $store.modals.close();
       }
     }"
     class="relative">

  <%# Brand sprite — MUST live inside this card's single root so the host's
      <template x-if> clones it along with the card. A sibling of the root is
      dropped and the icon paints empty. %>
  <%= render "studio/modals/blocks/wallet_brand_sprite" %>

  <div class="relative mb-1">
    <button @click="dismiss()" :disabled="connecting"
            class="absolute top-0 right-0 -mr-1 text-secondary hover:text-heading text-xl leading-none disabled:opacity-50"
            aria-label="Close">&times;</button>
  </div>

  <%= render "studio/modals/blocks/card_header",
        icon_emoji: "🔐",
        title: "Sign in with your wallet" do %>
    <p class="text-sm text-body">
      This account is secured by a Solana wallet. You are signed in, but this
      session can&rsquo;t sign on-chain &mdash; so entering contests and moving
      funds still need your wallet.
    </p>
  <% end %>

  <%# PRIMARY — one click when we remember the brand, the picker when we don't.
      THE STANDARD WEB3 AUTH BUTTON (operator call, 2026-08-21): a wallet row, not
      a filled CTA. Same shape the connect picker and the wallet-setup card use —
      brand mark, the wallet's own name, an Installed badge, chevron — so a wallet
      reads identically everywhere it is offered, and this card stops inventing a
      third look for the same action.

      It carries `pulse-cta` because it is the ONE target on this card and the
      whole point of the card is that the user should press it. Tuned to the same
      values the wallet-setup connect row uses, so the two beat alike rather than
      competing. %>
  <template x-if="canOneClick">
    <div>
      <button type="button" @click="signIn()" :disabled="connecting"
              style="--pulse-cta-color: rgb(var(--color-primary-rgb)); --pulse-cta-strength: 0.4; --pulse-cta-scale: 1.02"
              class="pulse-cta w-full flex items-center gap-3 p-3 rounded-xl bg-surface-alt border border-strong hover:bg-surface transition text-left disabled:opacity-60 disabled:cursor-wait">
        <span class="w-9 h-9 rounded-lg overflow-hidden flex items-center justify-center shrink-0">
          <svg class="w-9 h-9" aria-hidden="true"><use :href="'#se-wallet-' + provider"></use></svg>
        </span>
        <span class="font-semibold text-heading" x-text="providerLabel"></span>
        <span class="ml-auto flex items-center gap-2">
          <span x-show="connecting" class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 text-xs text-secondary"><span class="cta-spinner" aria-hidden="true"></span>Connecting&hellip;</span>
          <%# Detected: the same green Installed badge the connect picker and the
              wallet-setup row use. Absent until the poll finds the wallet, so the
              row never claims a presence it has not observed. %>
          <span x-show="!connecting && detected" class="badge border-primary text-primary">Installed</span>
          <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke-width="2" stroke="currentColor" class="w-4 h-4 text-muted">
            <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="m8.25 4.5 7.5 7.5-7.5 7.5" />
          </svg>
        </span>
      </button>
      <%# One line on what pressing it DOES. A signature prompt from a wallet is
          alarming without it, and "no funds move" is the difference between
          clicking and bailing — the same sentence the wallet-setup connect row
          carries. The address rides here so the user can confirm we are asking
          for the wallet they think we are. %>
      <p class="text-[11px] text-muted mt-1.5 px-1">
        Signing proves the wallet is yours &mdash; it does not move any funds.
        <template x-if="walletHint">
          <span>Wallet <span class="font-mono text-secondary" x-text="walletHint"></span></span>
        </template>
      </p>
    </div>
  </template>

  <%# No remembered brand — the same row shape with no mark to show, so the two
      halves of this card look like one card. %>
  <template x-if="!canOneClick">
    <button type="button" @click="openPicker()" :disabled="connecting"
            style="--pulse-cta-color: rgb(var(--color-primary-rgb)); --pulse-cta-strength: 0.4; --pulse-cta-scale: 1.02"
            class="pulse-cta w-full flex items-center gap-3 p-3 rounded-xl bg-surface-alt border border-strong hover:bg-surface transition text-left disabled:opacity-60">
      <span class="w-9 h-9 rounded-lg bg-inset flex items-center justify-center shrink-0 text-lg" aria-hidden="true">&#128091;</span>
      <span class="font-semibold text-heading">Connect your wallet</span>
      <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke-width="2" stroke="currentColor" class="ml-auto w-4 h-4 text-muted">
        <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="m8.25 4.5 7.5 7.5-7.5 7.5" />
      </svg>
    </button>
  </template>

  <template x-if="error">
    <p class="text-red-400 text-sm mt-3 text-center" x-text="error"></p>
  </template>

  <%# SECONDARY — always reachable, so a user whose remembered wallet is the
      wrong one is never cornered by our memory of it. %>
  <template x-if="canOneClick">
    <button type="button" @click="openPicker()" :disabled="connecting"
            class="mt-3 block w-full text-center text-sm text-secondary hover:text-heading disabled:opacity-50">
      Use a different wallet
    </button>
  </template>

  <div class="mt-4 pt-3 border-t border-strong">
    <button type="button" @click="dismiss()" :disabled="connecting"
            class="block w-full text-center text-sm text-secondary hover:text-heading disabled:opacity-50">
      Not now
    </button>
    <%# The escape hatch (operator call). A self-custody wallet is the one
        credential we cannot reset for the user, so the card must not end
        without a way to reach a human. %>
    <p class="mt-2 text-center text-[11px] text-muted">
      Can&rsquo;t access your wallet?
      <%= link_to "Get help", help_path, class: "underline hover:text-secondary" %>
    </p>
  </div>
</div>
